{"id":"services-networking-ingress","corpus":"k8s","title":"Ingress","sourceUrl":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/services-networking/ingress/","license":"Kubernetes Documentation © The Kubernetes Authors, licensed CC BY 4.0","licenseUrl":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","blocks":[{"kind":"h1","text":"Ingress"},{"kind":"p","text":"Make your HTTP (or HTTPS) network service available using a protocol-aware configuration mechanism, that understands web concepts like URIs, hostnames, paths, and more. The Ingress concept lets you map traffic to different backends based on rules you define via the Kubernetes API."},{"kind":"p","text":"An API object that manages external access to the services in a cluster, typically HTTP."},{"kind":"p","text":"Ingress may provide load balancing, SSL termination and name-based virtual hosting."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Kubernetes project recommends using Gateway instead of Ingress. The Ingress API has been frozen."},{"kind":"p","text":"This means that:"},{"kind":"li","text":"The Ingress API is generally available, and is subject to the stability guarantees for generally available APIs. The Kubernetes project has no plans to remove Ingress from Kubernetes."},{"kind":"li","text":"The Ingress API is no longer being developed, and will have no further changes or updates made to it."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Terminology"},{"kind":"p","text":"For clarity, this guide defines the following terms:"},{"kind":"li","text":"Node: A worker machine in Kubernetes, part of a cluster."},{"kind":"li","text":"Cluster: A set of Nodes that run containerized applications managed by Kubernetes. For this example, and in most common Kubernetes deployments, nodes in the cluster are not part of the public internet."},{"kind":"li","text":"Edge router: A router that enforces the firewall policy for your cluster. This could be a gateway managed by a cloud provider or a physical piece of hardware."},{"kind":"li","text":"Cluster network: A set of links, logical or physical, that facilitate communication within a cluster according to the Kubernetes networking model."},{"kind":"li","text":"Service: A Kubernetes Service that identifies a set of Pods using label selectors. Unless mentioned otherwise, Services are assumed to have virtual IPs only routable within the cluster network."},{"kind":"h2","text":"What is Ingress?"},{"kind":"p","text":"Ingress exposes HTTP and HTTPS routes from outside the cluster to services within the cluster. Traffic routing is controlled by rules defined on the Ingress resource."},{"kind":"p","text":"Here is a simple example where an Ingress sends all its traffic to one Service:"},{"kind":"p","text":"An Ingress may be configured to give Services externally-reachable URLs, load balance traffic, terminate SSL / TLS, and offer name-based virtual hosting. An Ingress controller is responsible for fulfilling the Ingress, usually with a load balancer, though it may also configure your edge router or additional frontends to help handle the traffic."},{"kind":"p","text":"An Ingress does not expose arbitrary ports or protocols. Exposing services other than HTTP and HTTPS to the internet typically uses a service of type Service.Type=NodePort or Service.Type=LoadBalancer."},{"kind":"h2","text":"Prerequisites"},{"kind":"p","text":"You must have an Ingress controller to satisfy an Ingress. Only creating an Ingress resource has no effect."},{"kind":"p","text":"You can choose from a number of Ingress controllers."},{"kind":"p","text":"Ideally, all Ingress controllers should fit the reference specification. In reality, the various Ingress controllers operate slightly differently."},{"kind":"p","text":"Make sure you review your Ingress controller's documentation to understand the caveats of choosing it."},{"kind":"h2","text":"The Ingress resource"},{"kind":"p","text":"A minimal Ingress resource example:"},{"kind":"p","text":"An Ingress needs apiVersion, kind, metadata and spec fields. The name of an Ingress object must be a valid DNS subdomain name. For general information about working with config files, see deploying applications, configuring containers, managing resources. Ingress controllers frequently use annotations to configure behavior. Review the documentation for your choice of ingress controller to learn which annotations are expected and / or supported."},{"kind":"p","text":"The Ingress spec has all the information needed to configure a load balancer or proxy server. Most importantly, it contains a list of rules matched against all incoming requests. Ingress resource only supports rules for directing HTTP(S) traffic."},{"kind":"p","text":"If the ingressClassName is omitted, a default Ingress class should be defined."},{"kind":"p","text":"Some ingress controllers work even without the definition of a default IngressClass. Even if you use an ingress controller that is able to operate without any IngressClass, the Kubernetes project still recommends that you define a default IngressClass."},{"kind":"h3","text":"Ingress rules"},{"kind":"p","text":"Each HTTP rule contains the following information:"},{"kind":"li","text":"An optional host. In this example, no host is specified, so the rule applies to all inbound HTTP traffic through the IP address specified. If a host is provided (for example, foo.bar.com), the rules apply to that host."},{"kind":"li","text":"A list of paths (for example, /testpath), each of which has an associated backend defined with a service.name and a service.port.name or service.port.number. Both the host and path must match the content of an incoming request before the load balancer directs traffic to the referenced Service."}],"lemmas":{"ingress":["ingres","ingress"],"ingres":["ingres","ingre"],"ingresses":["ingress","ingresses"],"networks":["network","networks"],"serviced":["serviced","service"],"servicing":["servicing","service"],"availables":["availables","available"],"using":["using","use"],"usings":["using","usings"],"used":["used","use"